4734: \begin{theorem}[Manual theorem-inventory boundary]
4735: \label{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}
4736: The file \path{theorem_inventory.json} is a manual Stage-A inventory record
4737: rather than a deterministic theorem-environment extractor output.  Its accepted
4738: role in the current theorem chain is finite obligation traceability: it
4739: enumerates the theorem-like obligations selected by the Stage-A review,
4740: including the reported count of sixty-two displayed theorem-like entries after adding the finite bad-record witness, and
4741: connects that finite list to the manifest and acceptance ledger.  The inventory
4742: indexes obligations; the evidence for those obligations is supplied by the
4743: corresponding TeX statements and proofs in this source, together with external
4744: records only where a statement explicitly cites them.  It is not an executable
4745: completeness certificate for all \LaTeX{} theorem environments in
4746: \path{main.tex}; no extractor command, extractor log, or theorem-environment
4747: coverage claim is part of the current submitted support surface.  Consequently
4748: any claim that the inventory is complete because a deterministic extractor found
4749: all theorem-like environments, or that the ledger itself semantically checks the
4750: printed proofs, is rejected by the current finite-record interface, while the
4751: weaker claim that it is a manual non-semantic ledger is accepted.  The current
4752: package may therefore cite the ledger only as an indexed support coordinate in
4753: the theorem chain of
4754: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, never as automated
4755: completeness, extractor soundness, or proof-checking evidence.
4756: \end{theorem}
4757: 
4758: \begin{proof}
4759: The support records named in Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger}
4760: are finite manifest and ledger records.  The root manifest exposes the path
4761: \path{theorem_inventory.json}; acceptance-ledger entry \path{a7} records the
4762: inventory, marker-existence, and submission-pack gates for the bounded
4763: theorem-ledger claim; and the certificate verifier checks only record shape,
4764: gate coverage, evidence pointers, and file availability.  None of these records
4765: names an extractor program, a command line that scans \path{main.tex} for
4766: theorem-like environments, or a log comparing extractor output with the inventory
4767: bytes.  By the command-result rule in the scope contract, such an executable
4768: completeness claim would require its command, source state, environment, exit
4769: code, and log path.  Those coordinates are absent.
4770: 
4771: The remaining accepted reading is exactly the manual one.  A finite manual
4772: inventory can still be useful: it fixes the Stage-A obligation surface that the
4773: submission chooses to expose and lets the certificate ledger prevent hidden
4774: prose-only references to that surface.  But because no deterministic extractor is
4775: recorded, the inventory cannot discharge the stronger claim that every theorem,
4776: proposition, lemma, corollary, or definition environment in the TeX source was
4777: independently discovered by an executable scan.  The printed mathematical
4778: obligations remain proved by the corresponding statements and proofs in this
4779: source, with external records serving as evidence only for the finite-record,
4780: command-boundary, manifest, and support-surface claims that cite them.  The
4781: inventory therefore performs only the indexing step: it lists the obligations and
4782: connects that list to the acceptance ledger, while proof and evidence remain on
4783: separate coordinates.  Thus \path{theorem_inventory.json} supplies only the
4784: bounded manual traceability coordinate stated in the theorem.  The stronger
4785: deterministic extraction reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord, namely
4786: the absent extractor command and log tuple.  The weaker manual-ledger reading has
4787: no such bad subrecord because its only asserted coordinates are the finite list,
4788: the manifest path, and the acceptance-ledger pointer.  Hence the finite interface
4789: accepts exactly the non-semantic manual boundary and rejects extractor
4790: completeness.
4791: \end{proof}
4792: 
4793: \begin{proposition}[Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure]
4794: \label{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure}
4795: Suppose a Stage-A pass regenerates either \path{theorem_inventory.json} or
4796: \path{theorem_inventory.md} after the package has selected the exact-inclusion
4797: support branch.  Then the final support-surface claims of
4798: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, the submitted theorem-ledger
4799: claim of Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, and the command
4800: boundary of Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory} remain closed
4801: only after the package refreshes the corresponding SHA-256 rows in
4802: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and reruns the finite record
4803: verifiers whose records name that digest manifest or inventory coordinate.  Once
4804: those rows and verifier logs are refreshed, the regeneration creates no new
4805: mathematical or executable extractor claim: it only changes the byte-coordinate
4806: of the manual theorem-obligation ledger inside the already selected finite
4807: support surface.
4808: \end{proposition}
4809: 
4810: \begin{proof}
4811: The selected support branch is byte-level exact inclusion.  In that branch,
4812: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} identifies the submitted support
4813: surface by relative paths together with the digest rows in
4814: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The theorem inventory is one of
4815: those paths: Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger} exposes
4816: \path{theorem_inventory.json} through the root manifest and acceptance-ledger
4817: entry \path{a7}, while the final digest record also lists the human-readable
4818: companion \path{theorem_inventory.md}.  Therefore regenerating either file after
4819: the digest record was written changes a finite coordinate used by the exact
4820: support-surface relation.  Until the SHA-256 rows are recomputed, the digest
4821: record denotes the old bytes, and the regenerated inventory is not the same
4822: finite certificate file as the one named by the final packaging record.
4823: 
4824: The required reruns are exactly the finite record verifiers at the package
4825: boundary.  The certificate-record verifier checks that the manifest-required
4826: paths, including the theorem-inventory coordinate, are present and covered by the
4827: accepted gate records; the source-interface and primary-claim verifiers record
4828: their command boundary against the same final digest manifest.  Rerunning those
4829: commands after the digest refresh supplies fresh source state, environment, exit
4830: code, and log-path coordinates for the command-boundary table.  No verifier in
4831: this list scans \path{main.tex} for theorem-like environments or proves theorem
4832: semantics.  Hence the refreshed state is closed precisely as a finite
4833: record-and-digest package, and by
4834: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} it remains a manual
4835: Stage-A obligation ledger rather than an executable theorem-environment
4836: extraction certificate.
4837: \end{proof}
4838: 
4839: \begin{corollary}[Current theorem-inventory digest instance]
4840: \label{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance}
4841: For the present Stage-A package, after \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
4842: \path{theorem_inventory.md} have been regenerated, the submitted
4843: theorem-obligation ledger may be cited in
4844: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} only through the refreshed
4845: digest rows for those two files in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}
4846: and through fresh finite-record verifier logs produced after the regenerated
4847: inventory files are present.  If either the two digest rows or the
4848: post-regeneration verifier logs are absent, the
4849: inventory remains a local manual index but is not closed as an exact-inclusion
4850: support coordinate for the bundle-backed theorem-ledger claim.
4851: \end{corollary}
4852: 
4853: \begin{proof}
4854: Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} to the
4855: two regenerated inventory files in this package.  The support branch selected by
4856: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} is exact inclusion, so the
4857: identity of each submitted support file is read from the corresponding
4858: relative-path and SHA-256 row in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.
4859: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} uses
4860: \path{theorem_inventory.json} only as the manual theorem-ledger coordinate, and
4861: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} excludes any stronger
4862: extractor or semantic-proof reading.  Therefore the only new fact needed after
4863: regeneration is byte-coordinate closure: the digest rows must name the new bytes,
4864: and the finite verifiers whose records mention the digest or inventory coordinate
4865: must be rerun against the package state in which the regenerated inventory files
4866: exist.  Without those rows and logs,
4867: the ledger is still a local manual file, but the exact-inclusion support relation
4868: points either to stale bytes or to an unverifiable command-boundary state.  With
4869: them, no additional mathematical, extractor, Lean, daemon, or artifact claim is
4870: introduced; the refreshed package supplies exactly the finite support coordinate
4871: used by the submitted theorem-obligation ledger.
4872: \end{proof}
